---
import Shell from '../../designs/Shell.astro';
import Command from '../../designs/Command.astro';
import Screen from '../../designs/Screen.astro';
import Quotes from '../../designs/Quotes.astro';
import Closing from '../../designs/Closing.astro';
import '../../designs/fieldnotes.css';
---
<Shell title="Fieldnotes · Your app, in good hands" theme="fieldnotes" number={3}>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="field-hero wrap">
      <div class="field-masthead"><p>AWS DevOps, fully automated.</p><span>Built for the team you have.</span></div>
      <h1>Your code deserves<br />a whole <em>DevOps team.</em></h1>
      <div class="field-intro">
        <p>
          Stacktape designs, deploys, and operates your application in your own AWS account. The work of a senior DevOps
          team, built into the way your developers already work.
        </p><Command />
      </div>
      <div class="field-trust">
        <span>Open-source CLI (MIT)</span><span>Extend with any AWS resource</span><span>Eject anytime</span>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div class="field-story wrap" id="product">
      <section class="field-chapter field-design" aria-labelledby="field-design-title">
        <div class="field-chapter-head">
          <p class="field-label">Design infrastructure</p>
          <h2 id="field-design-title">Start with your app.<br />The architecture follows.</h2>
          <p>
            Run Stacktape in your repository. It identifies what your app needs, writes the configuration, and shows you
            how everything fits together. You review the decisions before deploying.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Screen kind="design" />
        <div class="field-caption">
          <span>Generated configuration ↔ architecture</span><p>
            Private networking, scoped permissions, and sensible defaults.<br /><b>A complete starting point. Still
              completely yours.</b>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="field-chapter field-deploy" aria-labelledby="field-deploy-title">
        <div class="field-chapter-head">
          <p class="field-label">Deploy it</p>
          <h2 id="field-deploy-title">You push code.<br />Your users get the update.</h2>
          <p>
            Stacktape builds your containers and functions, creates the infrastructure, and handles the release. Updates
            roll out without downtime; pull requests get their own preview environments.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="field-delivery">
          <div class="field-git">
            <span class="field-small">YOUR WORKFLOW</span><strong>git push</strong><div
              class="git-lines"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <i></i><i></i><i></i>
            </div>
            <p>Same repository.<br />Same pull requests.<br />A lot less infrastructure work.</p>
          </div><Screen kind="deploy" />
        </div>
        <div class="field-caption">
          <span>Deployed in your AWS account</span><p>
            You own the resources.<br /><b>Roll back a release when you need to.</b>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="field-operations" aria-labelledby="field-operations-title">
        <div class="field-section-title">
          <p class="field-label">Operate it</p>
          <h2 id="field-operations-title">Going live is<br />just the beginning.</h2>
          <p>
            The same platform that understands your app’s infrastructure<br />helps you understand what happens in
            production.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="field-pair">
          <article>
            <p class="field-small">OBSERVABILITY</p>
            <h3>See what your<br />users experience.</h3>
            <p>
              Logs, metrics, traces, and uptime checks are connected from the first deploy. Follow a request across
              services and find what slowed it down.
            </p><Screen kind="observe" compact />
          </article>
          <article>
            <p class="field-small">INCIDENT RESPONSE</p>
            <h3>Fix the cause.<br />Verify the recovery.</h3>
            <p>
              Get the error, the release, and the evidence together. Your coding agent investigates; you review its fix.
              Automatic remediation stays opt-in.
            </p><Screen kind="incident" compact />
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="field-protection" aria-labelledby="field-protection-title">
        <div class="field-section-title">
          <p class="field-label">Protect it</p>
          <h2 id="field-protection-title">Good for your app.<br />Good for your business.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="field-pair">
          <article>
            <p class="field-small">SECURITY</p>
            <h3>Make safe the default.</h3>
            <p>
              Keep databases private, permissions scoped, and secrets managed. Enforce your team’s rules before a
              change reaches production.
            </p><Screen kind="security" compact />
          </article>
          <article>
            <p class="field-small">COST CONTROL</p>
            <h3>Keep the bill in view.</h3>
            <p>
              Understand spend by service and environment. Choose a budget and get a warning before you reach it. AWS
              bills you directly.
            </p><Screen kind="cost" compact />
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
    <Quotes />
    <Closing title="A small team can build a big thing." />
  </main>
</Shell>
